    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Greater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> was created under French Mandate on 1 September 1920, joining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a> with the north, the south and the Beqaa; the republic was proclaimed on 1 September 1926 and won its independence in November 1943.</li><li>The unwritten <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-pact">National Pact</a> of 1943 reserves the presidency for a Maronite Christian, the premiership for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and the speakership for a Shia; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taif-agreement">Taif Agreement</a> of 1989 made parliament's 128 seats equal between Christians and Muslims.</li><li>The civil war of 1975–1990 killed an estimated 150,000 people and displaced nearly a million; Syrian forces stayed until 2005 and Israel held the south until 2000, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezbollah">Hezbollah</a> has since outgunned the national army.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> has held no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> since 1932, when Christians were 53% of the population: the relative size of its religious communities is too sensitive for its confessional politics to count.</p></aside>
    </section>

