f-year">1943</span><span class="rf-text">The new government abolishes the mandate on 8 November and is imprisoned by the French; under British pressure the officials are released on 22 November and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> accepts <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>'s independence.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1975</span><span class="rf-text">Full-scale civil war breaks out between Christian groups and the forces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine-liberation-organization">PLO</a> and left-wing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> and Muslim militias.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1989</span><span class="rf-text">Parliament agrees the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taif-agreement">Taif Agreement</a>, with a timetable for Syrian withdrawal and equal Christian and Muslim representation; the war ends at the end of 1990.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2000</span><span class="rf-text">Israeli forces withdraw fully from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> in May; 25 May becomes Liberation Day.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2005</span><span class="rf-text">Former Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafic-hariri">Rafic Hariri</a> is assassinated on 14 February; mass demonstrations force <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> to withdraw its troops by 26 April.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2019</span><span class="rf-text">Mass protests from 17 October against sectarian rule, corruption and the liquidity crisis; the currency collapses and the state defaults in 2020.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2024</span><span class="rf-text">Israel invades on 1 October after killing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezbollah">Hezbollah</a>'s leader Hassan Nasrallah on 27 September; a ceasefire follows in November.</span></li></ol>
